🚀 Feature
Make the default collate a class instead of a function so that people can subclass for new types but can still use the default collate functionality.
Motivation
Currently, the default collate is a function that checks the type of the first element of the batch and recursively class itself when containers such as lists or tuples are used. When creating a custom collate function for a Dataset that returns multiple types this means that one has to implement all the types again.
Pitch
It would be useful to make the collate a class, which you can subclass.
class DefaultCollate:


  def __call__(self, batch):
      self.forward(batch)

  @staticmethod
  def forward(batch):
    r"""Puts each data field into a tensor with outer dimension batch size"""

    elem = batch[0]
    elem_type = type(elem)
    if isinstance(elem, torch.Tensor):
        # [...]
        return torch.stack(batch, 0, out=out)

    # [...]



class MyCollateThatSupportsNone(DefaultCollate):

    @staticmethod
    def forward(batch):

        elem = batch[0]

        if elem is None:
            return None
        else:
            return super().forward(batch)

Alternatives
The current "alternative" is, I suppose?, to copy and paste the default collate and add elif isinstance(elem, MyTYPE) for new custom types.

